<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sugita Genpaku</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1733">1733</a>–<a href="/years/1817">1817</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sugita Genpaku was a Japanese physician and scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rangaku">rangaku</a>, or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> learning. After watching a dissection in Edo in 1771 that confirmed the accuracy of a Dutch anatomy book, he and his colleagues translated it as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaitai-shinsho">Kaitai Shinsho</a>, published in 1774. His memoir, Rangaku Kotohajime, describes the difficulties of that work and became the founding story of Western learning in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sugita-genpaku.jpg" alt="Sugita Genpaku" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ishikawa Tairō · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sugita_Genpaku.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sugita Genpaku was born on 20 October 1733 in the Wakasa-Obama domain, the son of Hosen Sugita, official physician to the lord of Wakasa. His mother died at his birth. From about the age of 17 he studied surgery under Gentetsu Nishi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> under Saburoemon Miyase. At 25, with his lord's permission, he set up as a physician at Nihonbashidōri in Edo, and fires later forced him to move his practice twice, in 1759 and 1762.</p><p>He worked under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakoku">sakoku</a>, the policy that closed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> to most of the world and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a> the only port open to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> traders. The eighth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshimune">Tokugawa Yoshimune</a>, had begun the systematic study of Dutch in 1740, but Western books remained restricted. In 1768 Genpaku watched the Dutch surgeon Rudolf Bauer treat a patient with gangrene of the tongue, and he knew at second hand of Tōyō Yamawaki's dissection of 1754.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The dissection of 4 March 1771, attended with Maeno Ryōtaku, Hoshū Katsuragawa and Junan Nakagawa, was of an executed woman known as Aocha-Baba. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> atlas they compared with the body, Ontleedkundige Tafelen, went back to a German work by Johann Adam Kulmus of 1734. With only Maeno's vocabulary of some 600 Dutch words, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaitai-shinsho">Kaitai Shinsho</a> took at least eleven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and contained errors and omissions, yet it is regarded as a turning point for Western medicine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p>Genpaku taught many students of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rangaku">rangaku</a>, among them Gentaku Otsuki, Shojuro Arai and Genzui Udagawa. Otsuki rewrote the translation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaitai-shinsho">Kaitai Shinsho</a> in 1788. In his Keiei yawa Genpaku argued that apart from the difference between men and women there was no real distinction between human beings, from the emperor to the commoners, a view shared by other Tokugawa physicians but not widely accepted.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In old age Genpaku built a large medical library and spent more time as a physician than as a scholar. Seeking an heir, he took in a young student named Genshin, disowned him for his temper, and later accepted him again as his son and left him his estate. Genpaku died on 1 June 1817. His students formed a generation that brought Western medicine to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and his translations from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> were put into Chinese, among the first Western medical texts in that language.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sugita Genpaku” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1774 <b>Sugita Genpaku</b> and his colleagues published the Kaitai Shinsho, a translation of a Dutch anatomical text, and by the early nineteenth century rangaku scholars had prepared intellectual foundations for the rapid modernisation of the Meiji period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Yaraichō, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-tokyo">Edo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.7,139.73&amp;year=1787" aria-label="Yaraichō on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8078 874 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sugita Genpaku"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8528 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8528 1144h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.7,139.73&amp;year=1787">What was here in 1787? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
